# Building Synthetic Eval Sets for RAG Systems

## A Practical Strategy for Synthetic Generation, Realistic Noise, Multi-Document Coverage, and Diagnosable Scoring

---

## 1. Why Synthetic Eval Sets Matter

A RAG system has two failure surfaces — retrieval and generation — and each fails in different ways. Without a trusted evaluation set, teams iterate blindly: tuning chunking strategies, swapping embedding models, or rewriting prompts without knowing whether things got better or worse. A synthetic eval set is your ground truth. Every design decision gets measured against it.

The challenge is that most teams either skip eval entirely, or build a thin set of clean, obvious questions that flatters the system and hides real-world failure modes. Recent work formalizes this: Zhang et al. (2025) show that most existing RAG benchmarks assume error-free queries and dramatically overestimate real-world performance [QE-RAG]. Separately, SeedRG (arXiv:2605.08838, 2026) shows that many public benchmarks suffer from *knowledge leakage* — the model can answer the questions from parametric memory without any retrieval — meaning the benchmark doesn't actually test what it claims to.

This document lays out a complete strategy for building synthetic eval sets that are realistic enough to surface the problems that actually hurt users in production, along with a scoring framework that produces diagnosable, actionable numbers rather than opaque scores.

---

## 2. The Anatomy of a Synthetic Eval Record

Every record in your eval set should capture the full chain from question to evidence to answer. A minimal schema:

```json
{
  "id": "eval-0042",
  "question": "whats the return window for enterprise if they bought before jan",
  "question_type": "cross-doc-synthesis",
  "retrieval_pattern": "multi-chunk-cross-doc",
  "noise_profile": ["typo", "indirect-phrasing"],
  "gold_chunk_sets": [
    ["enterprise_terms.md#chunk4", "policy_update_jan2025.md#chunk2"],
    ["pricing_faq.md#chunk18"]
  ],
  "gold_answer": "Enterprise customers who purchased before January 2025 have a 60-day return window under the legacy terms. Purchases after January 1, 2025 follow the new 30-day policy.",
  "intent_points": [
    {"id": "P1", "text": "60-day window for pre-Jan-2025 purchases", "required": true},
    {"id": "P2", "text": "30-day window for post-Jan-2025 purchases", "required": true},
    {"id": "P3", "text": "Notes this is the 'legacy' vs 'new' policy distinction", "required": false}
  ],
  "unanswerable": false,
  "requires_synthesis": true,
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "domain_tags": ["policy", "enterprise", "temporal"],
  "scoring_notes": "Must mention both the 60-day legacy and 30-day new policy. Partial credit if only one is mentioned."
}
```

Key fields beyond the obvious:

- **gold_chunk_sets** — a list of *alternative* valid passage sets, not just one. This prevents penalizing correct retrieval that found a different valid path.
- **intent_points** — an atomic decomposition of what the answer must convey. Each point is tagged as required or preferred. This drives partial-credit scoring and connects directly to the binary decomposition scoring in Section 6.
- **retrieval_pattern** — categorizes the structural difficulty so you can slice metrics.
- **noise_profile** — records what realism transformations were applied, so you can measure robustness per noise type.
- **scoring_notes** — captures rubric edge cases that a generic "correct/incorrect" label would lose.

