## 3. Question Type Taxonomy

Your eval set should cover these categories deliberately, not accidentally. Aim for rough proportions tuned to your actual usage, but ensure every category has enough examples (minimum 10–15) to spot patterns.

### 3.1 Single-Chunk Factual Lookup
The answer lives in one passage. "What is the maximum file upload size?" This is the easiest case and where most RAG systems score well. Don't over-index on these — they'll inflate your numbers.

### 3.2 Multi-Chunk, Same Document
The answer requires combining information from different sections of the same document. "Summarize the eligibility criteria and the application process." The system needs to retrieve multiple chunks and the generator needs to weave them together.

### 3.3 Multi-Chunk, Cross-Document
The answer requires synthesizing information scattered across separate documents. "How does the enterprise SLA compare to the standard plan?" One doc defines enterprise terms, another defines standard. This is where most RAG systems break down, and where your eval set earns its value. Shen et al. (2025) show that most automated question generators produce "pseudo multi-hop" questions that are actually answerable from a single document; genuine cross-doc questions require bridge-entity identification across complementary documents [HopWeaver].

### 3.4 Unanswerable / Out-of-Scope
The corpus genuinely does not contain the answer. The correct behavior is abstention or a clear "I don't have information on this." Allocate 10–20% of your eval set to these. Systems that never say "I don't know" will hallucinate on these, and you need to catch that.

### 3.5 Ambiguous / Multi-Interpretation
The question has more than one reasonable reading. "What's the limit?" Limit on what — file size, API calls, users? These test whether the system asks for clarification or picks the most common interpretation. Annotate the acceptable interpretations.

### 3.6 Comparative
"How does X differ from Y?" These naturally require cross-document retrieval and structured generation. They're common in real usage and disproportionately hard.

### 3.7 Procedural / How-To
"How do I reset my API key?" These require retrieving step-by-step instructions and preserving their order. Partial retrieval (getting steps 1, 2, and 4 but missing step 3) is a distinct failure mode.

### 3.8 Adversarial / Distractor-Heavy
Questions where a naive keyword match retrieves a plausible-looking but wrong passage. "What's the cancellation policy for the Pro plan?" and the corpus has cancellation policies for Free, Pro, and Enterprise — the system needs to not blend them.

### 3.9 Contradiction / Conflict Resolution

Two or more documents in the corpus give conflicting answers to the same question — often because one is outdated, one is regional, or they were authored by different teams. The correct behavior is to surface the conflict rather than blend or arbitrarily pick one. Example: an HR policy doc says PTO carries over year-to-year; a newer benefits amendment says it caps at 40 hours.

This category is often skipped in synthetic eval work, but it's disproportionately valuable because production corpora accumulate contradictions naturally. Annotate the gold answer to explicitly note the conflict and, if applicable, the resolution policy (prefer newer, flag to user, defer to authority document).

