## 9. Beyond RAG Q&A: Adjacent Evaluation Surfaces

This document focuses on evaluating RAG *question answering*. For agentic systems that use retrieval as one capability among many, several adjacent evaluation surfaces need their own treatment. They're briefly noted here so you know what this document does and doesn't cover.

**Tool routing evaluation.** For systems that route queries across multiple tools or MCP servers, whether the right tool was invoked is a separate evaluation surface from whether retrieval within that tool worked. Metrics include tool selection accuracy, tool precision (fraction of invoked tools that were needed), tool recall (fraction of required tools invoked), and tool hallucination rate (attempts to call nonexistent tools). The binary decomposition methodology in Section 6.2 transfers directly — routing dimensions can be decomposed into binary questions like "Was the correct MCP server selected?" or "Was the tool sequence in the right order?" Vocabulary mismatch (Section 4.2 Layer 1) matters even more for routing than for retrieval, because tool descriptions are the retrieval target.

**Cross-knowledge-base synthesis.** When answers require combining information from structurally different sources (SQL databases, document corpora, APIs), each source may need its own eval subset plus an integration eval that tests synthesis quality across sources. The cross-doc synthesis category (Section 3.3) is a subset of this; the general case requires additional annotation of which knowledge base each fact should come from.

**Multi-turn conversation.** The eval records in this document are single-turn. Extending to multi-turn requires new schema fields (turn history, resolved references, context carryover annotations) and new binary questions (did the answer correctly resolve pronoun references from prior turns? did it preserve prior context? did routing behavior remain stable?). Long-session degradation, context drift, and self-consistency across turns are all distinct failure modes worth their own evaluation.

**End-to-end business workflow evaluation.** Question-level eval is a necessary but not sufficient signal for whether a system delivers business value. Workflow-level eval measures whether a chain of interactions — retrieval, synthesis, follow-up, action — successfully completes a real business task. This is closer to integration testing than to the methodology in this document.

**Production monitoring.** Retrieval drift (does retrieval quality degrade as the corpus grows or shifts?), routing accuracy in production, hallucination rate on live queries, data freshness, and user satisfaction each need their own monitoring pipelines. Offline eval predicts production performance imperfectly; production monitoring closes the loop.

These are complementary to the synthetic Q&A eval strategy in this document, not replacements for it. A robust agentic system likely needs all of them layered together.

---

## 10. Maintenance and Versioning

### 10.1 Version Control

Treat the eval set as code. Store it in version control with semantic versioning. Every change — adding questions, updating gold answers, revising chunk annotations — gets a version bump and a changelog entry.

### 10.2 Staleness Detection

When documents in your corpus are updated, flag all eval questions whose gold chunks reference modified documents. Re-validate: is the gold answer still correct? Are the gold chunks still the right passages? Do new passages now also answer this question?

Automate this with a dependency tracker: map each eval question to its gold chunk document IDs, and trigger a review whenever those documents change.

### 10.3 Expansion Cadence

Add 10–20 new questions per quarter, prioritizing:
- Question types where current performance is weakest
- New document domains added to the corpus
- Real user queries that your system failed on in production (the best source of realistic, high-value eval cases)

### 10.4 Retirement

Remove or archive questions when:
- The source document has been permanently removed from the corpus
- The question was found to be ambiguous or poorly annotated after repeated disagreements
- The system has scored 100% on it for five consecutive eval runs (it's no longer differentiating — replace with something harder)

