## 11. Research Landscape

The following summarizes recent verified work on RAG evaluation and synthetic benchmark construction that informs the strategy in this document.

**BINEVAL (Cho et al., 2026, arXiv:2606.27226)** — the theoretical grounding for Section 6.2. BINEVAL decomposes evaluation criteria into atomic binary questions and demonstrates on SummEval, Topical-Chat, and QAGS benchmarks that this approach yields higher rank correlation with human judgment than holistic G-Eval and UniEval, avoids ceiling effects, and produces score distributions more closely matching humans. Critically for our purposes, the paper also shows that the same question-level feedback supports iterative improvement of the evaluator prompt itself — the diagnostics in Section 6.3 are motivated by this insight.

**HopWeaver (Shen et al., 2025, arXiv:2505.15087, ACL 2026)** — the state-of-the-art approach to cross-document multi-hop question synthesis. HopWeaver introduces a pipeline for identifying bridge entities across complementary documents and constructing genuine multi-hop reasoning paths, avoiding the "pseudo multi-hop" problem where LLM-generated multi-hop questions are actually answerable from single documents. Cross-doc question generation in Section 4.1 should use this approach.

**SeedRG / Leakage-Free Benchmarks (arXiv:2605.08838, 2026)** — formalizes *knowledge leakage* (when questions are answerable from parametric memory alone) as a benchmark validity criterion. Reports leakage rates of 52% on HotpotQA, 62% on 2WikiMultihopQA, and 75% on QASC. Proposes a semi-synthetic pipeline using reasoning graph extraction with type-constrained entity replacement to reduce leakage. **Implication:** for any corpus that includes public content, run each eval question through the LLM with no retrieved context and discard questions it answers correctly.

**RAGAS (Es et al., 2024, EACL, arXiv:2309.15217)** — the foundational reference-free RAG evaluation framework. Introduces faithfulness (proportion of claims supported by retrieved context), answer relevance, and context precision/recall metrics, all implemented without gold references. RAGAS also supports synthetic test data generation. Their claim-level faithfulness scoring is a lightweight predecessor of the binary decomposition approach; use RAGAS metrics as complementary aggregate signals alongside the more diagnostic binary decomposition.

**ARES (Saad-Falcon et al., 2024, NAACL, arXiv:2311.09476)** — automates RAG evaluation using fine-tuned lightweight LM judges plus prediction-powered inference (PPI). Requires only ~150 human-annotated samples for calibration but provides statistical confidence intervals on automated metrics. **Implication:** when scores need to be defensible to stakeholders or when you want to detect statistically-meaningful improvements between system versions, use ARES-style PPI calibration on top of binary decomposition.

**QE-RAG (Zhang et al., 2025, arXiv:2504.04062)** — the first RAG benchmark specifically evaluating robustness to query entry errors. Documents that even simple keyboard-proximity typos substantially degrade RAG performance on benchmarks where models otherwise score well. Provides a taxonomy of realistic error patterns (keyboard proximity, visual similarity, spelling mistakes) that directly informs Section 4.2 Layer 2.

**RAGXplain (Cohen, Burg, Barkan, 2025, arXiv:2505.13538)** — quantifies RAG performance and translates raw scores into narrative explanations with actionable recommendations, using LLM reasoning to identify performance gaps and suggest targeted improvements. Reports that LLM-based metric assessments show strong alignment with human judgments and that applying RAGXplain's recommendations measurably improves system performance on public QA datasets. **Relation to this document:** binary decomposition (Section 6.2, following Cho et al., 2026) produces the fine-grained diagnostic signal; RAGXplain-style narrative synthesis is a complementary layer on top that turns those verdicts into engineering recommendations. Consider adopting both together: the binary layer for interpretability and error localization, the narrative layer for prioritization and communication with non-technical stakeholders.

**EnterpriseRAG-Bench (Sun et al., 2026, arXiv:2605.05253, Onyx + UC Berkeley)** — a public benchmark consisting of a synthetic 500,000-document enterprise corpus spanning nine source types (Slack, Gmail, Linear, Google Drive, HubSpot, Fireflies, GitHub, Jira, Confluence) and 500 questions across ten categories. Their question taxonomy — including single-document lookup, multi-document reasoning, constrained retrieval, conflict resolution, and recognizing absent information — closely parallels our Section 3 taxonomy and validates the coverage model. **Scale caveat:** 500 questions across 500K documents is a ratio of 1:1,000, far sparser than what this document recommends for internal use. Their eval is designed for a public leaderboard where sparse coverage over a large synthetic corpus is appropriate; for internal diagnostic evals over a real corpus, the recommendations in Section 7 (roughly 250–370 questions for 1K docs) provide roughly 100× denser per-document coverage.

**Brehme, Ströhle, Breu (2026, arXiv:2604.18234, SynIRgy Workshop at ECIR 2026)** — introduces *Context-Aware Retriever Evaluation* (CARE), showing that for multi-hop queries, evaluators must consider the full set of retrieved contexts rather than assessing each in isolation. Individual chunks may appear irrelevant on their own while being jointly relevant when combined. Experiments on HotpotQA, MuSiQue, and SQuAD with LLM judges from OpenAI, Meta, and Google demonstrate CARE consistently outperforms isolated-context evaluation. **Practical implication:** this validates our set-level recall metric (Section 5.2) over chunk-level recall for cross-doc questions, and suggests that when using LLM-as-judge for binary decomposition (Section 6.2), the full retrieved context should be presented together rather than question-by-question against individual chunks.

**BenchmarkQED (Microsoft, open-source at github.com/microsoft/benchmark-qed)** — an open-source toolkit with three components: *AutoQ* generates synthetic queries spanning local (small text regions) to global (dataset-wide reasoning) scope, and supports data-linked multi-hop local queries via named entities; *AutoE* evaluates RAG answers using LLM-as-judge with metrics for relevance, comprehensiveness, diversity, and empowerment, plus assertion-based scoring; *AutoD* provides dataset sampling and summarization utilities. The local/global query distinction is a useful axis to add to the question taxonomy in Section 3 if your users perform both quick lookups and dataset-wide analytical questions.

