## 12. Quick Reference — Red Flags in Your Eval Set

| Red Flag | What It Means |
|---|---|
| >80% of questions are single-chunk lookups | You're not testing real-world difficulty |
| 0% unanswerable questions | You can't detect hallucination |
| All questions use source-document vocabulary | You're not testing retrieval robustness |
| No typos or fragments | Your scores won't predict production performance |
| Only one gold chunk set per question | You're penalizing valid alternative retrievals |
| No contradiction or distractor cases | You're ignoring a major class of production errors |
| Holistic 1–5 scoring instead of binary decomposition | You can't diagnose which claim types fail or why |
| No eval set quality checks (Section 6.4) | Bad gold annotations silently corrupt your metrics |
| Same binary questions fail >50% of the time across all records | Your binary question may be too strict or poorly worded |
| Eval set hasn't changed in 6 months | It's probably stale |
| Public-source corpus with no leakage check | Your benchmark may be measuring memory, not retrieval |
| Questions never fail — 100% pass rate on some records for many runs | Those records aren't differentiating; retire them |

---

## 13. End-to-End Workflow Summary

1. **Chunk your corpus** and build a chunk index with stable IDs.
2. **Generate clean question-answer pairs** from chunks using an LLM, covering the question types in Section 3.
3. **For cross-doc questions**, use bridge-entity identification (HopWeaver-style) rather than naive multi-document prompting.
4. **If corpus includes public content**, run a leakage check — discard questions answerable from parametric memory alone.
5. **Apply realism transformations** (Section 4) with tracked noise profiles.
6. **Annotate gold chunk sets** including alternative valid retrieval paths, and intent decomposition (atomic points).
7. **Add unanswerable, adversarial, contradiction, and wrong-assumption questions** manually.
8. **Have a second annotator review** all records.
9. **Define binary decomposition questions** for both answer scoring (Section 6.2) and eval set quality scoring (Section 6.4).
10. **Run eval set quality checks** using binary decomposition (Section 6.4). Fix flagged records.
11. **Stratify and balance** the final set across all dimensions (Section 7).
12. **Run RAG evals using binary decomposition** (Section 6.2) — score answers per dimension, aggregate failures to find systemic patterns.
13. **Version, store, and maintain** as a living artifact (Section 10).
14. **Slice by category and by failed binary question**, and use the breakdowns to decide where to invest next.

For teams building their first eval set, start with the MVP in Section 8 and grow from there.

